{\*\generator Adobe Acrobat Exchange-Pro 8.1.2}{\info{\title Microsoft Word - $ASQ4277558_File000005_64174755.doc}{\author ActivePDF - ActivePDF}{\creatim\yr2008\mo7\dy11\hr17\min8\sec0}{\revtim\yr2008\mo9\dy9\hr9\min22\sec20}{\id1306664}}\pard\plain\ql\f0\fs24\cf0 
\paperh15840\paperw12240\margt1400\margr1160\margl1357\pard\s1\absw10097\phpg\posx1440\pvpg\posy1440\qc\fs23 Review of \ldblquote Cultural Taste Diversity ad Network Activation: Reconstructing the Functional Role \ulnone\strike0\par\pard\s2\absw2734\phpg\posx1440\pvpg\posy1992\sl553\slmult0\fs23 
of Cultural Capital\rdblquote  \ulnone\strike0\par\pard\s2\absw9417\phpg\posx1440\pvpg\posy3096\sl553\slmult0\fs23 Summary: The paper extends recent work in the sociology of culture and social network research that has established a link between network embeddedness and the diversity of cultural consumption.  It argues, persuasively, that in order for social capital to have functional advantages to the individual, social ties must be \ldblquote activated\rdblquote  in some way, for example to learn about and take advantage of opportunities for employment (pp. 2-3).  A case is made that cultural capital, especially in the form of diversity of cultural consumption, plays a role in facilitating the creation, maintenance, and activation of economically valuable social networks (pp. 6-7).  This link, in which cultural capital is \ldblquote converted\rdblquote  into social capital, is one part of a two-way feedback process, the other being the tendency of social interaction to increase cultural similarity 
\ulnone\strike0\par\pard\absw8876\phpg\posx1440\pvpg\posy8064\ri0\sl553\slmult0\fs23 (p.\fs23  8).  A recent paper by Lizardo is cited which provides empirical evidence supporting the feedback model.  \ulnone\strike0\par\pard\s3\absw9333\phpg\posx1440\pvpg\posy9168\fi0\sl553\slmult0\fs23 
In the present paper, it is further hypothesized that (1) diversity of cultural tastes increases the likelihood of network activation in job searches versus non-network strategies, (2) culturally influenced network activation is more likely to tap weak as opposed to strong ties, and 
\ulnone\strike0\par\pard\s2\absw9358\phpg\posx1440\pvpg\posy10824\sl553\slmult0\fs23 (3) \fs23 consumption diversity reduces the duration of unemployment, by way of the mechanisms specified in the first two hypotheses.  The hypotheses are tested using a subset of 540 unemployed respondents from a representative U.S. survey who were actively seeking employment.  The results show that (1) respondents who either contacted friends and relatives or contacted a potential employer directly in order to find work had engaged in more cultural 
\ulnone\strike0\par\pard\absw9219\phpg\posx1440\pvpg\posy13584 activities and practices during the previous year than respondents who used other strategies, (2) \ulnone\strike0\par\pard\pagebb\absw8896\phpg\posx1440\pvpg\posy1440 respondents who contacted an employer directly had more cultural activities than those who 
\ulnone\strike0\ulnone\strike0\par\pard\absw9247\phpg\posx1440\pvpg\posy1992\ri0\sl553\slmult0\fs23 contacted friends and family, and (3) the duration of unemployment decreased as the number of cultural activities increased, in a Poisson regression model. 
\ulnone\strike0\par\pard\s3\absw9396\phpg\posx1440\pvpg\posy3096\fi0\sl553\slmult0\fs23 Assessment: The topic addressed by the paper is important.  If it could be shown that cultural consumption practices influence the duration of unemployment by affecting the likelihood and nature of network activation, the contribution to knowledge would be undeniable.  Unfortunately, there are a number of serious problems that prevent me from accepting the author\rquote s conclusions at face value. 
\ulnone\strike0\par\pard\s3\absw9415\phpg\posx1440\pvpg\posy5856\fi0\sl553\slmult0\fs23 One problem concerns the measurement of network activation.  Although contacting friends and family seems like an adequate measure of strong-tie activation on its face, contacting a prospective employer directly is surely not a valid measure of weak-tie activation.  The assertion that \ldblquote persons are much more likely to have direct access to an employer through a weak tie\rdblquote  (p. 12) is insufficient to validate the item as a measure of weak-tie activation, since there is no indication whatsoever that pre-existing networks were involved.  Respondents may have simply answered an job advertisement or looked up a desired employer in the phone book.  If so, then the results would actually show the 
\i\fs23\f1 opposite\i0\fs23\f0  of what the author claims, namely, that cultural diversity increases the likelihood of \i\fs23\f1 direct\i0\fs23\f0  contact with an employer compared to (strong-tie) network activation or to other non-network strategies. \ulnone\strike0\par\pard\absw9256\phpg\posx1440\pvpg\posy11376\fi0\ri0\sl553\slmult0\fs23 
A second problem concerns the nature of the association between cultural variety and network activation.  Even if we assume that the measures are valid, the logistic regression of network versus non-network strategies on cultural variety, controlling for gender, race, education, age, and prestige of previous job, does not suffice to allay concerns that the association between cultural diversity and network activation is non-spurious (p. 13).  Although 
\ulnone\strike0\par\pard\s1\pagebb\absw9688\phpg\posx1440\pvpg\posy1440\qc\fs23 it was found in a previous study that \ldblquote as the number of cultural activities that the respondent \ulnone\strike0\par\pard\s2\absw9374\phpg\posx1440\pvpg\posy1992\sl553\slmult0\fs23 
engages in increases so does the expected size of their personal network\rdblquote  (p. 8), this finding does not by itself demonstrate that cultural diversity actually \i\fs23\f1 increases\i0\fs23\f0  network size in a causal way.  To be sure, it does seem plausible that cultural skills, knowledge, and tastes do play a gate-keeping role in facilitating entry into certain bounded social networks.  For example, if I wished to join a particular network of young urban professionals whose informal interaction rituals included discussing extreme sports, alternative popular music, and independent film, then my chances of success were no doubt increase greatly if I happened to possess the cultural knowledge needed to take part in discussions of these topics.  Once entry is achieved, it is quite plausible that I would have access to information about job opportunities that I might otherwise lack in the event I became unemployed.  By extension (as the author suggests), 
\i\fs23\f1 diversity\i0\fs23\f0  of cultural knowledge would be expected to increase the likelihood of access to any given network and, hence, of being able to activate the relevant social ties to find a job.  \ulnone\strike0\par\pard\s3\absw9381\phpg\posx1440\pvpg\posy8616\fi0\sl553\slmult0\fs23 
Nonetheless, I am not at all convinced that cultural diversity \i\fs23\f1 directly \i0\fs23\f0 explains network size and hence the likelihood of network activation strategies, let alone that \ldblquote cultural diversity \u8230 \'85 is the \i\fs23\f1 only
\i0\fs23\f0  useful predictor\rdblquote  of choosing a socially embedded strategy (p. 14; author\rquote s italics).  Both cultural diversity and network activation could be due other, confounding factors like individual propensity toward sociability, prior network embeddedness, or some other unmeasured source of heterogeneity.  Moreover, even if the relationship were truly causal, I see no reason why it should be expected to affect the formation 
\i\fs23\f1 familial\i0\fs23\f0  networks.  (Maybe this is one reason why cultural variety has no significant effect on the activation of strong ties in Table 4?).  \ulnone\strike0\par\pard\s3\pagebb\absw9396\phpg\posx1440\pvpg\posy1440\fi0\sl553\slmult0\fs23 
Finally, the way some of the regression models are specified is a bit puzzling.  For one thing, in Table 4 network versus non-network strategy is regressed on cultural variety with and without controlling for gender, races, age, education, and prestige of last job.  But no controls are included for the weak-tie and strong-tie models.  Would the \ldblquote effect\rdblquote  of cultural variety on weak-tie activation still be significant with the controls included?  For another, the Poisson regression of length of unemployment on cultural variety is expressly motivated by the hypothesis that the latter affects the former by way of its effect on network activation (p. 15).  If so, then one might have expected one or more network activation variables to be included as independent variables (implicitly, as mediators) at some stage of the analysis.  If the effects of cultural variety are mediated by network activation, then the coefficient for the former should be significant when the mediator is omitted and become insignificant when it is included.  Without this theoretically crucial step, however, the model seems to imply that cultural variety directly reduces length of unemployment, a scenario that seems very unrealistic.  
\ulnone\strike0\par\pard\s3\absw9347\phpg\posx1440\pvpg\posy8616\fi0\sl553\slmult0\fs23 In sum, in order to persuade me that the findings and conclusions are well-founded, a revised version of the paper would need to (1) establish that directly contacting a prospective employer is a valid measure of network activation, (2) do a better job of justifying the hypothesized causal relationship between cultural variety and network activation, (3) show that the coefficient for cultural variety in Table 4, model 3, is still significant when the controls are included, and that none of the controls does a better job of predicting network activation than does cultural variety, and (4) show that network activation has the hypothesized mediating effect on duration of unemployment in model 5.  This is a tall order, but given the author\rquote s obvious technical and theoretical sophistication, perhaps it is not beyond reach. 
\ulnone\strike0 }